# 040 - `!=` across two different types is the same Type error as `==`
# (§6/.40). The landed value.rs exposes a single entry point (eq_value), so a
# correct engine must negate it and the message names `==` even for `!=`. Bead
# .39 proposes eq_op(ne: bool) instead, which would change these bytes; see the
# spec-gap bead filed with this corpus.
--- source
print 1 != "1";
--- expect
error: Type at line 1: == expects two values of the same type, got Int and Str
